<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cacique">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cacique</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as cazique, cacica, kasike</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cacique (feminine cacica) was a chief of certain Indigenous peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>; the word comes from the Kalinago kassiquan, 'to keep house'. Among the Taíno the rank was hereditary. The Spanish used the word for the leader of almost any Indigenous group: early Caribbean caciques such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a> and Enriquillo eventually revolted, while those of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became loyal intermediaries of colonial rule, styled don and doña, and some held entailed estates. Mexican independence in 1821 abolished their last privileges; the word later also came to mean a local political boss.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cacique.jpg" alt="Cacique" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acuarela_de_Túpac_Amaru_II_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word cacique, sometimes spelled cazique, with the feminine form cacica, comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> word kassiquan, which means to keep house. It first entered English in 1555, defined as prince. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> the kasike rank was hereditary and sometimes established by democratic means. A kasike's importance depended on the size of his clan rather than on skill in war. Kasikes lived in a larger rectangular hut at the centre of the village and had reserved places from which to watch the ceremonial dances and ball game. The kasike's word was law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spanish took over the term and used it for the local leader of almost any Indigenous group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. Caribbean caciques who did not at first resist became middlemen between their communities and the Spanish, but their cooperation was often provisional. Most of the early caciques revolted and died in battle or by execution. Two of the best known are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a>, from what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, and Enriquillo, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>.</p><p>The Spanish had more success with the leaders of the more hierarchical societies of Central Mexico, who became loyal intermediaries in the colonial system. Cooperative elite men often replaced rivals with better hereditary claims. The Spanish treated Indigenous nobles as a European-style nobility: caciques and their families were allowed the honorifics don and doña, and gained rights to carry swords or firearms and to ride horses or mules. Some held entailed estates called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacicazgo">cacicazgos</a>, and many engaged in sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> raising. By the late colonial period the title had lost any dynastic meaning, and the last special privileges were abolished with Mexican independence in 1821.</p><p>In the Andean region the local term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">kuraka</a> was preferred, and the Spanish let kurakas keep their titles if they swore fealty to the Spanish monarch. The 1781 rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> was led by a kuraka claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> royal line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In Hispanic and Lusophone countries the word also came to mean a political boss, like a caudillo, exercising power in a system of caciquism. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> after independence it kept its meaning of Indigenous leader but also came to mean any local or regional leader. Caciquismo appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, and Benedict Anderson coined the term cacique democracy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Scholars do not agree on the origins of caciquismo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cacique” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chumayel chronicle speaks of the multepal, the 'joint government' in the city, and a colonial report describes Mayapan as a kind of court where all the <b>caciques</b> and lords of the land resided and brought their tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Muisca government rested on chiefs, whom the Spanish called <b>caciques</b>, and on the kin-based communities beneath them, which the sources call uta and sybyn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish control rested on alliances with native <b>caciques</b>, who allied with the Spanish to strengthen their power, gain protection against rivals and acquire goods; these alliances were unstable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The early alliance custom of cuñadazgo, by which <b>caciques</b> gave women to newcomers, was supplanted after 1541 by the rancheadas, mass seizures of women who were bought, sold and exported as slaves; modern historians describe this as sexual and economic coercion.</blockquote><a href="/culture/guarani">Guaraní · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Taíno lived in villages, called yucayeques, under a chief known as a <b>cacique</b>, or cacica when the ruler was a woman, and grouped their chiefdoms, the cacicazgos, into confederations.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The word <b>cacique</b>, which Spanish conquerors applied to native rulers throughout the Americas, was borrowed from the Taíno, whose chiefdoms Columbus met in the Caribbean in 1492.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
